WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.771
Debugging is the hidden cost.

2
00:00:02.051 --> 00:00:04.227
A workflow fails at a step you can name.

3
00:00:04.507 --> 00:00:11.355
An agent fails along a trajectory: a sequence of choices, each reasonable-looking, that ends somewhere wrong.

4
00:00:11.635 --> 00:00:29.299
Anthropic’s multi-agent post describes the problem from production: agents make dynamic decisions and are non-deterministic between runs even with identical prompts, so a user’s report that the agent “didn’t find obvious information” can’t be reproduced by rerunning it.

5
00:00:29.579 --> 00:00:37.963
Small changes can cascade into large behavioral changes, and one failed step can send an agent down an entirely different path.

6
00:00:38.243 --> 00:00:50.915
Their answer was engineering: full production tracing of agent decisions, the ability to resume from the point of failure rather than restart, and careful rollout of changes so in-flight agents weren’t broken.

7
00:00:51.195 --> 00:00:52.880
Count that work in your estimate.

8
00:00:53.160 --> 00:01:06.088
An agent you can operate needs trace logging of every model call and tool call, trajectory-level evals with repeated trials (Studio 3, lesson 11), budgets, and someone who reads transcripts.

9
00:01:06.368 --> 00:01:10.101
A workflow needs a log line per step and a unit test per branch.

10
00:01:10.381 --> 00:01:15.587
If the task doesn’t need the agent’s flexibility, that operational cost buys nothing.
